+/* The RC8650's CTS# pin (datasheet: OUTPUT, "Low when the RC8650 is able to
+   accept data...  CTS# acknowledges each byte received") is P6_0, driven at
+   0x03ec.  The firmware raises it while busy (init, and in the receive wake
+   handler as a per-byte acknowledge) and lowers it when ready for the next
+   byte.  Model a host that honours CTS#: present a byte in U0RB -- and raise
+   RI / the receive request -- only while CTS# is low.  Without this, every
+   queued byte lands during boot, before the firmware arms its receive path,
+   and the "wake on first byte" handler never fires (see notes.md).
+   M32C_RX_NOCTS=1 restores the old fire-hose behaviour.
+
+   Once a byte has been presented (RI shown / request raised) it stays in U0RB
+   until read, regardless of CTS# -- the receive wake handler raises CTS#
+   *before* it reads U0RB.  Presentation is also edge-triggered: the request
+   bit is raised once per byte, not continuously, otherwise the interrupt
+   re-fires between vectoring and the U0RB read and a phantom 0x00 lands in
+   the receive ring.  */
